= Concept =

== Overview ==
A '''concept''' is a unit or constituent of thought or structured meaning used in categorization, inference, memory, learning, communication and knowledge organization.

Concepts help cognitive agents and information systems group particulars, recognize patterns and reason about kinds of things.

The nature of concepts is disputed. Philosophers have treated concepts as mental representations, abilities, abstract objects or other structures.

== Concepts and words ==
A concept is not the same thing as a word.

Different words can express roughly the same concept.

One word can also express several concepts depending on context.

For example, "bank" can refer to a financial institution or the side of a river.

Therefore:

<pre>
word / label ≠ concept
</pre>

Labels are ways of naming or expressing concepts.

== Concepts and categories ==
A [[Category]] groups items under some organizing distinction.

A concept can provide the meaning used to define or recognize a category.

But "concept" and "category" are not identical in every theory.

A concept can participate in reasoning without corresponding to one clean extension of categorized objects.

== Concepts and entities ==
A [[Entity|entity]] is something represented or referred to.

A concept can be about a kind of entity.

For example:

<pre>
concept: planet
entity: Mars
</pre>

Mars is not the concept Planet.

Confusing these levels creates modeling errors in knowledge systems.

== Concepts as mental representations ==
One influential view treats concepts as mental representations.

On this account, concepts are internal representational structures that participate in:
* recognition;
* memory;
* reasoning;
* language;
* decision-making.

Different psychological theories propose prototype, exemplar, theory-like, symbolic or distributed representations.

== Concepts as abilities ==
Ability-based theories treat possessing a concept partly as having capacities to discriminate, infer, classify or use expressions appropriately.

This avoids assuming that every concept is a stored internal symbol.

It also emphasizes competence and use.

== Concepts as abstract objects ==
Another tradition treats concepts as abstract entities such as Fregean senses.

On this view, different thinkers can grasp the same concept because concepts are not identical with private mental states.

The challenge is explaining how minds stand in relation to such abstract objects.

== Concept structure ==
Some concepts appear to have definitional structure.

Others appear prototype-like or theory-like.

For example, mathematical concepts can often be defined with high precision, while biological/social concepts can have fuzzy or contested boundaries.

A single universal theory of concept structure remains controversial.

== Concepts in knowledge organization ==
Knowledge organization systems often represent concepts explicitly.

The W3C Simple Knowledge Organization System (SKOS) defines <code>skos:Concept</code> as a flexible resource representing an idea or notion in a concept scheme.

SKOS concepts can have:
* preferred labels;
* alternative labels;
* notes;
* broader/narrower relations;
* related-concept links;
* mappings to other concept schemes.

SKOS deliberately avoids committing to one philosophical theory of concepts.

== Concepts and classes ==
Formal ontology languages often use '''classes''' to denote sets/categories of individuals satisfying class conditions.

A concept and a formal class can correspond in some systems, but they should not be identified by definition.

A concept can remain vague, contested or pragmatically defined while a formal class requires explicit semantics.

== Concept schemes ==
A concept scheme is an organized collection of concepts and semantic relationships.

Examples include:
* thesauri;
* subject-heading systems;
* taxonomies;
* controlled vocabularies.

Concept schemes usually support indexing and navigation more than heavy logical inference.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki uses Concept as a local entity type and also has a MediaWiki Concept namespace for semantic query structures.

Those implementation roles should not be mistaken for a universal definition of a concept.

